    "response": "It is understandable to feel frustrated after reaching the final shortlist of three for a senior leadership role and not receiving the outcome—or the feedback—you hoped for. \n\nHowever, **you should be very cautious about drawing conclusions from the gender composition of the panel, and you should not make an issue of it.** \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why, along with a look at what likely drove the decision and how best to proceed:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition Does Not Prove Bias\n* **Panel makeup is usually role-driven:** In corporate banking, senior hiring panels are composed of specific internal stakeholders—for example, the Chief Risk Officer, a Head of Compliance or Regulatory Affairs, a peer business head, and an HR/Talent director. The fact that these four individuals happened to be women is most often a reflection of who holds the relevant stakeholder positions, rather than an intentional demographic alignment.\n* **Lack of evidentiary basis:** Just as an all-male interview panel (which has historically been very common in finance) does not automatically demonstrate unlawful discrimination against female candidates, an all-female panel does not demonstrate discrimination against male candidates. \n* **High professional risk:** Raising allegations or insinuations of gender bias without concrete evidence (such as documented discriminatory statements or procedural breaches) is nearly impossible to substantiate. Doing so can severely damage your professional reputation and burn bridges in what is often a small, interconnected risk-modelling community.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \"Head of\" Roles Prioritize Different Skills Than Technical Roles\nA common point of friction for quantitative specialists transitioning to executive levels is the shift in required competencies:\n\n* **Technical Depth vs. Strategic/Regulatory Breadth:** \n  A PhD in statistics is an asset for technical model development. However, for a **Head of Risk Modelling** at a regulated bank, day-to-day work is often less about mathematical derivation and far more about:\n  * Navigating regulatory frameworks (e.g., Basel III/IV, CCAR, CECL, IFRS 9, TRIM).\n  * Direct interaction with regulators (e.g., PRA, ECB, Fed, OSFI) and defending models during supervisory audits.\n  * Translating complex quantitative outputs into actionable risk insights for the board, CRO, and non-technical business leaders.\n* **The Winning Profile:** \n  The announcement highlighting \"broad experience of regulatory affairs\" and a master’s in finance suggests the bank placed a premium on regulatory interface and business acumen. If the regulatory scrutiny on the bank’s models is currently high, someone with proven regulatory standing often edges out someone with deeper technical/statistical depth.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Written Feedback\n* **Standard corporate latency:** Two weeks is very normal in bank HR timelines. Once a preferred candidate accepts, HR's focus shifts immediately to onboarding, background checks, and contract finalization.\n* **Legal sensitivity around written feedback:** Banks are often cautious with written post-interview feedback for executive positions, and notes frequently require review or sign-off by HR or internal counsel before being released.\n* It is rarely a sign of bad faith; it is usually simply bureaucracy.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a Professional, Forward-Looking Follow-Up:**\n   Follow up politely with the recruiter or hiring manager. Frame your request around personal development rather than contesting the decision:\n   > *\"I understand you are busy finalizing the recruitment process, but I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback from the final round. I remain very interested in leadership opportunities in this area and would greatly value any insights the panel had on where I can strengthen my profile—particularly around strategic or regulatory capabilities.\"*\n\n2. **Shift Focus to Regulatory & Executive Experience:**\n   If you want to secure a \"Head of\" position in the near term, identify any gaps in your profile regarding regulatory negotiation, model governance frameworks, and high-level stakeholder management.\n\n3. **Protect Your Relationships:**\n   Making the final three out of what was likely a large candidate pool means the bank viewed your technical expertise and qualifications very favorably. Keeping this relationship cordial leaves the door open for future executive roles or recommendations.\n",
